---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About - Molinello Music" description="Molinello is a small Utah workshop run by bluegrass player Boyd Timothy, building gear that helps acoustic musicians sound like themselves on stage.">
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
      <h1 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-4xl md:text-5xl mb-12 text-center">About</h1>

      <div class="md:flex gap-12 items-start mb-12">
        <div class="md:w-2/5 mb-8 md:mb-0 space-y-4">
          <img src="/images/chester/chester-v2-mounted-angle.jpg" alt="Chester microphone mounted on an upright bass" class="rounded-xl shadow-lg w-full" />
          <img src="/images/about/molinello-amps.jpg" alt="Molinello hand-built tube amplifiers" class="rounded-xl shadow-lg w-full" />
        </div>
        <div class="md:w-3/5 space-y-6 text-gray-700 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I'm Boyd Timothy. Before anything else, I'm a player. I play guitar, mandolin and upright bass in Basin &amp; Grange, a bluegrass band out of Salt Lake City.
          </p>
          <p>
            Molinello began as a record label. When the band finished our debut album, <a href="/records" class="text-brand-orange hover:underline"><em>Walk You Down the Road</em></a>, we needed a name to release it under. I borrowed one from
            <a href="https://www.molinello.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brand-orange hover:underline">Il Molinello</a>*,
            a small agriturismo in the hills of Tuscany where my wife and I once stayed. One of the owners was an avid cyclist, like me, and the place left a lasting mark on how I think about craft and doing things with care.
          </p>
          <p>
            Playing out, I kept running into the same problem: acoustic instruments rarely sound like themselves through a PA. Pickups sound plugged in. Clip-on mics are flimsy, and good studio mics don't survive a moving player. So I started building the gear I needed. Chester, an upright bass microphone, came out of that, and AcoustiMatch, software that makes a pickup sound more like a mic, came after it.
          </p>
          <p>
            I've also built my share of electric gear: tube guitar amps from kits and from scratch, and pedals like the Q-Tune tuner and The Flick. Those builds taught me most of what I know about circuits, and they're still some of my favorite time at the bench.
          </p>
          <p>Everything is built by hand in Highland, Utah. Thanks for stopping by.</p>
          <p class="font-heading font-bold text-brand-dark">Boyd Timothy</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 italic">
            *I have no affiliation with them, but it was an excellent place to vacation!
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Past builds -->
      <div id="past-builds" class="scroll-mt-20 border-t border-gray-200 pt-10 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="font-heading font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Past builds</h2>
        <div class="sm:flex gap-6 items-start">
          <img src="/images/milton/milton-on-stand.jpg" alt="Milton large-diaphragm condenser microphone on a stand" loading="lazy" class="w-40 rounded-lg shadow mb-4 sm:mb-0 shrink-0" />
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-heading font-semibold text-brand-dark">Milton.</span>
            Before Chester there was the Milton, a large-diaphragm condenser for acoustic instruments and vocals. It's retired now, but the
            <a href="https://youtu.be/youoGAF5FXM" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brand-orange hover:underline">build video</a>
            is still up.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
